1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,700
If your prompts sound like

2
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:14,450
"help me with work"

3
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,200
and the answer comes back like a motivational poster —

4
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:21,950
you're not bad at AI.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,700
You're asking for magic

6
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:29,450
instead of giving instructions.

7
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,200
Today: one idea, one skill, one action.

8
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:36,950
A prompt structure that still works when the logos change.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,950
Picture this.

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,950
You finally open ChatGPT after work in Soweto, Polokwane,

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,950
Durban — wherever home is —

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,950
and you type: "Help me with my report.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,950
"

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,950
It gives you a generic essay.

15
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,950
You think: "Maybe I'm not smart enough for this.

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,950
"

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,950
A cousin sends a "secret prompt" with twenty roles and a pirate voice.

18
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,950
You try it. Still fluff. Still shame.

19
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,950
I've watched brilliant women conclude they lack a talent

20
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,950
when what they lacked was a clear ask.

21
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,950
Here's the truth:

22
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,950
models follow instructions.

23
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,950
Vague instructions produce vague help.

24
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,950
The problem isn't that AI is useless.

25
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,950
The problem is we treat prompting like wishing.

26
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,950
Wishing sounds like: "make this better.

27
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:38,950
"

28
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:46,950
Instructing sounds like: who you are for this task, what to

29
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:54,950
produce, what context matters, what limits to respect, and

30
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,950
what good looks like.

31
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,950
So let's name the durable parts:

32
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:18,950
Role — who should the model act as for this job.

33
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:26,950
Task — the deliverable in one clean verb phrase.

34
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:34,950
Context — the facts it must know.

35
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,950
Constraints — length, tone, format, what to avoid.

36
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:50,950
Example — a tiny sample of the shape you want.

37
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,950
Tricks expire.

38
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,950
Clear instructions compound.

39
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,950
Here's the skill.

40
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,950
Framework name: RTCCE

41
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,950
Role — Task — Context — Constraints — Example.

42
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,950
Watch a rewrite.

43
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,950
Bad: "Help with work email."

44
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,950
Better:

45
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,950
Role: You are a concise workplace writing assistant for South

46
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,950
African professionals.

47
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,950
Task: Turn these notes into a follow-up email confirming next steps.

48
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,950
Context: Project Alpha meeting today; next check-in Thursday 10:

49
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,950
00; recipient is my manager Thandi.

50
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,950
Constraints: Maximum 120 words; warm professional tone; no

51
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,950
buzzwords; South African English.

52
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:30,950
Example: Open with the decision made, then one clear ask.

53
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,950
Run it.

54
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:42,950
Edit the facts.

55
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:48,950
Save the prompt that worked in a note titled Forever Prompts.

56
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,950
That is RTCCE.

57
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,950
You stop gambling on magic words —

58
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,950
and you start writing instructions your future self can reuse.

59
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,617
Quick psychology — not as a diagnosis, as a tool.

60
00:06:13,667 --> 00:06:20,283
Shame grows in unclear tasks.

61
00:06:20,333 --> 00:06:26,950
When the output is bad and the ask was vague,

62
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:33,617
people blame identity: "I'm bad at this.

63
00:06:33,667 --> 00:06:40,283
"

64
00:06:40,333 --> 00:06:46,950
Clarity relocates the problem to the instruction —

65
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:53,617
which you can improve.

66
00:06:53,667 --> 00:07:00,283
Clarity reduces shame.

67
00:07:00,333 --> 00:07:06,950
Shame shrinks when the next prompt is specific.

68
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,664
Your action today — under thirty minutes:

69
00:07:12,714 --> 00:07:18,379
1. Find one prompt that gave you fluff.

70
00:07:18,429 --> 00:07:24,093
2. Rewrite it with Role, Task, Context, Constraints, and Example.

71
00:07:24,143 --> 00:07:29,807
3. Run it once, human-edit the result, and save the winning

72
00:07:29,857 --> 00:07:35,521
prompt in Notes.

73
00:07:35,571 --> 00:07:41,236
When you're done, you will not need a new tool.

74
00:07:41,286 --> 00:07:46,950
You will own a FutureHer skill that outlasts the hype cycle.

75
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,950
Download the free RTCCE Prompt Card,

76
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,950
join this week's AI challenge,

77
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,950
and subscribe so we can keep building calm fluency together.

78
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,950
The Forward Collective is practising one saved prompt a day.

79
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:15,950
Next time: fact-check AI before it embarrasses you —

80
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:24,950
because fluency is not proof.
